{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '205,380p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '435,510p'; tail -22 docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md; rg -n 'macro-defined|source-macro|harvest|declaration order' docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '205,380p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '435,510p'; tail -22 docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md; rg -n 'macro-defined|source-macro|harvest|declaration order' docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md\"","aggregated_output":"   205\t :eval     (fn [lambda-ast env prelude budget] → value)   ; §3.3\n   206\t :t        0}                     ; batch counter; incremented once per expand-batch\n   207\t```\n   208\t\n   209\t`expand-batch`:\n   210\t\n   211\t1. **Admit.** The batch is indexed with `index-datoms` and checked as a\n   212\t   whole — **every entity in the index, not only those reachable from the\n   213\t   root**, because harvest (step 2) reads disconnected definitions: every\n   214\t   `:yin/type` is in the closed vocabulary (§3.3), every ref resolves to an\n   215\t   entity in the index, the whole graph is acyclic, every ground value is\n   216\t   plain data, and every `:macro? true` lambda is the value operand of a\n   217\t   `(yin/def <literal sym> …)` — a macro lambda anywhere else has no meaning\n   218\t   on either side of the boundary and, forwarded, would load on the walker\n   219\t   as a plain closure, the quiet case decision 5 exists to prevent — all\n   220\t   *before* any recursive decoding (Appendix B). An in-memory medium can\n   221\t   carry host values, and the sandbox's closed-input guarantee holds only if\n   222\t   nothing else is admitted. A failure here is\n   223\t   `{:status :error :error {:kind :malformed-input :reason :unknown-type |\n   224\t   :dangling-ref | :cyclic | :host-value | :stray-macro-lambda :eid e}}`\n   225\t   carrying a **batch-level\n   226\t   event** (§4.1) — allocated from the watermark alone, since the index may\n   227\t   be unusable — and the advanced `:ctx`. Diagnostics name entities, never\n   228\t   carry the offending values.\n   229\t2. **Harvest.** `definitions` finds every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the\n   230\t   batch, in batch order, last wins: `v` a `:macro? true` lambda →\n   231\t   `assoc` into `:store`; `v` anything else (a plain lambda, a variable, a\n   232\t   literal) → `dissoc`, so a redefinition as a non-macro stops expansion of\n   233\t   that name. Harvest is *syntactic* and whole-batch by policy: a definition\n   234\t   in an unexecuted branch, or after its first use in the same batch, is\n   235\t   still in force for the whole batch. (Clojure defines a macro only when the\n   236\t   `defmacro` form executes; this design does not, and says so.)\n   237\t3. **Replace.** Each macro definition node is replaced by a **stand-in**\n   238\t   `{:type :yin/macro-defined :name <sym>}` — an internal node type of the\n   239\t   expander's working representation, not of any evaluator. It carries its\n   240\t   identity by *shape*, so it survives every transformation a macro can\n   241\t   apply to it: a macro that receives it as an operand and returns it, or\n   242\t   wraps it, hands back the same shape, and nothing in the pipeline needs\n   243\t   an eid or `m` to recognise it. A macro that fabricates one gains nothing\n   244\t   (step 5: it only re-asserts a source definition that already exists).\n   245\t   Step 7 lowers every stand-in to `{:type :literal :value <sym>}` for\n   246\t   `program-out`, so a batch whose root is a definition still forwards a\n   247\t   complete program and a REPL prints the name. Plain `yin/def`s are\n   248\t   forwarded untouched.\n   249\t4. **Expand.** `expand-node` (§3.2) runs from the root over the index.\n   250\t5. **Post-harvest.** Expansion can *form* definitions that did not have the\n   251\t   `yin/def` shape in the source — `(defn m …)` above all, but also an\n   252\t   operator that rewrites into `yin/def` — and copying along changed paths\n   253\t   gives nodes fresh eids and `default-op`, so neither eid nor `m` can say\n   254\t   where a definition came from. Step 5 therefore does not ask. The store\n   255\t   for the **next** batch is recomputed as: start from the store *as it was\n   256\t   before step 2*; walk the **final tree** in **declaration order** (below);\n   257\t   at every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` occurrence, a macro lambda `v`\n   258\t   `assoc`es and anything else `dissoc`es; at every `:yin/macro-defined\n   259\t   <sym>` stand-in, `assoc` the source macro `<sym>` that step 2 harvested\n   260\t   from *this* batch (a stand-in naming no such macro — fabricated — is\n   261\t   ignored). Last occurrence in declaration order wins. Position is a\n   262\t   property of the tree, not of the node, so re-parenting cannot lose it;\n   263\t   a definition formed by rewriting is present in the tree like any other;\n   264\t   and a stand-in is recognised by shape, so passing through a macro cannot\n   265\t   lose it either.\n   266\t\n   267\t   **Declaration order** is *not* the expansion traversal order of §3.2\n   268\t   (which is operator-first so that fresh ids are deterministic). yang\n   269\t   lowers `(do A B)` to `((fn [_] B) A)` and `(let [x v] body)` to\n   270\t   `((fn [x] body) v)`; operator-first would visit B before A. Declaration\n   271\t   order is: for an `:application` whose operator is a `:lambda`, the\n   272\t   operands left to right, then the lambda's body; for every other node,\n   273\t   its children in the fixed order. This recovers source sequence order for\n   274\t   every `do`/`let` nesting yang produces, and is deterministic for\n   275\t   everything else. **Ordering rule:** step 2's\n   276\t   source-only store governs the whole current batch; step 5's result\n   277\t   governs from the next batch. Consequences, stated: `(def m f)` then\n   278\t   `(defmacro m …)` keeps the macro (its stand-in is later in declaration\n   279\t   order); `(identity-macro (defmacro m …))` keeps it (the stand-in comes\n   280\t   back by shape);\n   281\t   `(defmacro m …)` then `(defn m …)` or `((choose-def) 'm f)` removes it\n   282\t   (the formed plain definition is later); a source macro definition that\n   283\t   an enclosing macro *discards* from its output is in force for the current\n   284\t   batch (step 2) and absent from the next (not in the final tree) — the\n   285\t   final tree is the program; within one batch a macro `m` and a `(defn m\n   286\t   …)` both apply to their own forms in source order, as Clojure would not.\n   287\t   The design accepts these one-batch divergences in exchange for a\n   288\t   one-pass, deterministic rule that needs no origin tracking.\n   289\t6. **Mark tail.** `mark-tail` recomputes `:tail?` over the whole final tree\n   290\t   (§3.4).\n   291\t7. **Emit.** Every `:yin/macro-defined` stand-in is lowered to\n   292\t   `{:type :literal :value <sym>}`; output is then emitted bottom-up as a\n   293\t   self-contained batch with a fresh `:yin/root` fact; the log copy carries\n   294\t   `m` per §4.1. No evaluator ever sees the internal node type.\n   295\t8. **Scan.** A final walk asserts no lambda with `:yin/macro? true` remains.\n   296\t   With admission rejecting stray macro lambdas, step 3 replacing source\n   297\t   definitions, and `:generated-macro` rejecting generated ones, this cannot\n   298\t   fire; it is the guard for decision 5 and a violation is a forwarder\n   299\t   defect, thrown as `{:kind :scan-failed :eid e}`.\n   300\t\n   301\tAn `:error` result carries the event (§4.1) in `:log` and the advanced\n   302\t`:ctx`; `:datoms` is absent. `expand` unwraps `:ok` and throws on `:error`\n   303\tfor callers that want the exception. Same inputs, same output, on every\n   304\thost.\n   305\t\n   306\t**Working representation.** Traversal, cycle detection, and shadow tracking\n   307\trun over *eids in the index*. Plain AST maps are materialised only at the\n   308\tboundary of one body invocation — operands decoded for `invoke`, the returned\n   309\tvalue validated and re-encoded to datoms with fresh ids, and re-expansion\n   310\tcontinues over the index of the batch-so-far. Internal identity never\n   311\tappears in the map representation macros see (`valid-ast?` rejects `:eid`).\n   312\t\n   313\t**Allocation.** `:alloc` is seeded per batch to\n   314\t`(min (:next-eid alloc) (dec min-eid-in-batch) (- (inc datom/first-user-id)))`\n   315\t— the incoming watermark is a term, so the counter only ever decreases across\n   316\ta session and event eids on the accumulated log never collide. It is returned\n   317\tadvanced in both `:ok` and `:error` results; a retry of a staged flush reuses\n   318\tthe staged allocation and never allocates again.\n   319\t\n   320\t### 3.2 The algorithm\n   321\t\n   322\tTraversal visits ref attributes in a **fixed order** so fresh ids and\n   323\tgensyms are deterministic across hosts: `:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`\n   324\t(left to right), `:yin/test`, `:yin/consequent`, `:yin/alternate`,\n   325\t`:yin/body`, `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node`, `:yin/source`. This order is\n   326\tfor allocation determinism only; it is not source order (§3.1 step 5's\n   327\tdeclaration order is). `shadow` is the set of names bound by enclosing\n   328\t`:lambda :params`.\n   329\t\n   330\t```\n   331\tmacro-of ctx eid shadow :\n   332\t  case operator(eid)\n   333\t    :variable v  → v ∉ shadow ∧ (:store ctx) v\n   334\t    :lambda  l   → l when (:macro? l)     ; defensive only: unreachable after admission (§3.1) and :generated-macro\n   335\t    _            → nil\n   336\t\n   337\texpand-node ctx eid shadow depth :\n   338\t  if type(eid) = :application ∧ (m = macro-of ctx eid shadow):\n   339\t      ev        = fresh-event ctx eid m                  ; allocated first: every failure below is recorded on ev\n   340\t      if depth ≥ :max-depth: fail ev {:kind :depth-guard :depth :chain}\n   341\t      operands  = [decode c | c ∈ operands(eid)]         ; unexpanded AST maps\n   342\t      out       = invoke m operands ctx                  ; §3.3 — arity, fuel, suspension fail on ev\n   343\t      check valid-ast? out {:allow-macro-lambda? false}  ; node count ≤ :max-nodes-per-expansion\n   344\t      root'     = encode out (fresh ids from :alloc)     ; joins the index of the batch-so-far\n   345\t      record ev :yin/expansion-root root'\n   346\t      return expand-node ctx root' shadow (depth+1)      ; re-expand the output under the call site's scope\n   347\t  else if type(eid) = :application:\n   348\t      op'       = expand-node ctx operator(eid) shadow depth        ; operator FIRST\n   349\t      eid'      = if op' = operator(eid) then eid else copy eid with operator op'\n   350\t      if macro-of ctx eid' shadow:\n   351\t          return expand-node ctx eid' shadow depth        ; became a macro call: takes the first arm with the ORIGINAL operands\n   352\t      operands' = [expand-node ctx c shadow depth | c ∈ operands(eid)]\n   353\t      return if operands' = operands(eid) then eid' else copy eid' with operands'\n   354\t  else:\n   355\t      shadow'   = if type(eid) = :lambda then shadow ∪ params(eid) else shadow\n   356\t      children' = [expand-node ctx c shadow' depth | c ∈ children(eid) in fixed order]\n   357\t      return if children' = children(eid) then eid else copy eid with children'\n   358\t```\n   359\t\n   360\t`fail ev …` writes `:yin/error` on the event and unwinds to `expand-batch`,\n   361\twhich returns the `:error` result with that event in `:log`. Cycle and\n   362\tdangling-ref detection happened at admission (§3.1 step 1), so this walk is\n   363\tover a known-acyclic graph; output encoded from validated plain maps is\n   364\tacyclic by construction.\n   365\t\n   366\tThe application branch expands the **operator before the operands** so that\n   367\tan operator which rewrites into a macro name hands that macro its operands\n   368\t*as written*: in `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with `choose` → `defn`, `defn`\n   369\tmust receive `(x 1)` unexpanded so the lambda it builds can shadow `x`.\n   370\tExpanding operands first would violate outermost-first for exactly the\n   371\tmacros (binders) that depend on it. Recognition is not an expansion — the\n   372\tre-entry is at the same depth — and a successful re-check goes straight into\n   373\tthe invocation arm, which re-expands at `depth+1`, so a chain of generated\n   374\tapplications consumes depth and cannot rewrite forever at constant depth.\n   375\t\n   376\tProperties, each a Phase 1 test:\n   377\t\n   378\t- **Outermost first.** A macro sees its operands as written. This is what\n   379\t  makes `->`-style macros expressible and what retires the shadow hints:\n   380\t  in `(defn f [x] (x 1))`, `defn` expands *before* its operands are examined,\n   435\t            (seq (:ready-queue vm)) (throw (ex-info \"Macro body scheduled work\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   436\t            (vm/halted? vm)         (vm/value vm)\n   437\t            :else                   (recur (vm/step vm) (inc n))))))\n   438\t```\n   439\t\n   440\tThis is a private detail of the expander, not a VM feature: the throwaway\n   441\tVM is an ordinary evaluator running an ordinary lambda. It has no\n   442\t`:make-stream` and no FFI pair, so stream and FFI effects fail with the\n   443\texisting constructor errors; `yin/def` inside a body writes to the throwaway\n   444\tstore and is discarded; `require` resolves against `nil` modules and fails.\n   445\tThe body sees only its parameters, the prelude, and `yin.vm/primitives` —\n   446\tdecision 6. Macro authors are told plainly: *a macro body may not call a\n   447\tfunction the program defined, and may not call another macro: the body is\n   448\texecuted, not expanded, so a macro name inside it is an unbound variable.\n   449\tHelpers for macros are prelude calls.* Expanding macro bodies before\n   450\tstoring them (which would allow macro helpers) is reserved (§7).\n   451\t\n   452\t`:eval` and the prelude are trusted composition capabilities under a stated\n   453\tcontract: deterministic, no host IO, no retention of arguments. The fuel\n   454\tbudget bounds VM transitions, not the cost of one host primitive or the\n   455\ttraversal of one large literal; `:max-nodes-per-expansion` bounds AST nodes,\n   456\tnot literal payload size. Both are known limits of v1, recorded here rather\n   457\tthan solved: a composition that admits untrusted macro *authors* needs a\n   458\tpayload bound at admission (§3.1 step 1), which is where such a bound\n   459\tbelongs.\n   460\t\n   461\t\"Run a lambda under a step budget in a fresh VM\" is the one capability the\n   462\texpander needs that `yin.vm` does not export today. It is a general\n   463\tfacility — any process that runs another's code wants it — and is the\n   464\treason the expander can later become a `yin.vm` program itself (§7,\n   465\tReserved) without any macro-specific primitive.\n   466\t\n   467\t**Validation.** The same `valid-ast?` runs on what goes *in* (every operand\n   468\tAST decoded for a body, and every ground value at admission) and on what\n   469\tcomes *out* (the body's value): a closed, plain-data Universal AST. It\n   470\tenforces, per node, exactly the vocabulary `ast->datoms-with-root` emits:\n   471\t\n   472\t| `:type` | Required | Optional |\n   473\t|---|---|---|\n   474\t| `:literal` | `:value` plain data | `:tail?` |\n   475\t| `:yin/macro-defined` | `:name` symbol | — (expander-internal, §3.1 step 3; accepted in operands and output, never emitted) |\n   476\t| `:variable` | `:name` symbol | `:tail?` |\n   477\t| `:lambda` | `:params` vector of symbols, `:body` | `:tail?` `:macro?` |\n   478\t| `:application` | `:operator`, `:operands` vector | `:tail?` |\n   479\t| `:if` | `:test` `:consequent` `:alternate` | `:tail?` |\n   480\t| `:dao.stream.apply/call` | `:op` keyword, `:operands` | `:tail?` |\n   481\t| `:vm/gensym` | | `:prefix` string |\n   482\t| `:vm/store-get` | `:key` | |\n   483\t| `:vm/store-put` | `:key` `:val` plain data | |\n   484\t| `:vm/park`, `:vm/current-continuation` | | |\n   485\t| `:vm/resume` | `:parked-id` keyword, `:val` | |\n   486\t| `:stream/make` | | `:buffer` int |\n   487\t| `:stream/put` | `:target` `:val` | |\n   488\t| `:stream/cursor`, `:stream/next`, `:stream/close` | `:source` | |\n   489\t\n   490\tAny other `:type`, any key outside the row, any `:eid`, a `:macro? true`\n   491\tlambda where `:allow-macro-lambda?` is false, or any non-plain value\n   492\tanywhere (recursively through vectors, maps, and sets, including inside\n   493\t`:literal :value`) is `{:kind :invalid-output :macro eid :at [path]}` (or\n   494\t`:malformed-input` at admission).\n   495\t*Plain data* is nil, booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, symbols, and\n   496\tvectors/maps/sets thereof. `:vm/store-update` is absent: it is a walker-only\n   497\ttransition with no datom form. The walk also counts nodes for the\n   498\tper-expansion bound, checked *before* emission so oversized output never\n   499\tmaterialises.\n   500\t\n   501\t**The prelude** — primitives merged into the throwaway VM for one body:\n   502\t\n   503\t| Primitive | Meaning |\n   504\t|---|---|\n   505\t| `yin/node-type ast`, `yin/node-get ast k` | accessors |\n   506\t| `yin/literal v`, `yin/variable sym` | leaf constructors |\n   507\t| `yin/lambda params body`, `yin/application op operands`, `yin/if t c a` | node constructors |\n   508\t| `yin/sequence-body [ast ...]` | the `do` desugaring yang uses; empty → `nil` literal |\n   509\t| `yin/make-lambda params-ast body-ast` | `params-ast` is a `:literal` whose value is the param vector |\n   510\t| `yin/make-def name-ast value-ast` | `(yin/def <sym> value)`; `name-ast` may be `:variable`, `:literal` symbol, or `:literal` string |\n\nRound 4 (`collab/1789279972076-review-macro-stream-process-r4.{gpt-6-astra,glm-5.3}.findings.md`), on revision 4; glm approved r4:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 28 | Post-harvest removes a later winning source macro because an earlier plain source `yin/def` survives in the tree (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 is a unified last-wins over source definitions at their positions and *generated* definitions (identified by `m` = event, never eid) at their call sites' positions; consequences enumerated; four acceptance cases plus re-parented variants in Phase 1 |\n| 29 | Stray source macro lambda outside a definition shape has no disposition (glm P3-1) | §3.1 step 1 `:stray-macro-lambda` at admission; step 8 named `:scan-failed` as a forwarder defect |\n| 30 | Appendix C row 5 names the retired `:generated-definition` (glm P3-2) | Row 5 annotated |\n\nRound 5 (`collab/1789280260256-review-macro-stream-process-r5.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`), on revision 5:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 31 | `m` is emission provenance, not definition origin: a re-parented generated definition carries `default-op`, and an operator rewrite forms a `yin/def` under `default-op`; both escape post-harvest (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 ranks every `yin/def` occurrence in the *final tree* in traversal order, with step 3's recorded stand-in literals (`:replaced`) representing source macro definitions; no origin tracking, no use of eid or `m`; discarded-definition consequence stated; both examples in Phase 1 |\n| 32 | `macro-of`'s inline macro-lambda arm is unreachable after admission (astra P3) | §2.3 drops the inline case as supported input; §3.2 marks the arm defensive |\n\nRound 6 (`collab/1789280492428-review-macro-stream-process-r6.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`), on revision 6:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 33 | Operator-first traversal visits `((fn [_] B) A)` as B then A, inverting `do`/`let` declaration precedence (astra P2-1) | §3.1 step 5 defines **declaration order** separately from expansion traversal: immediately-applied lambdas rank operands before body; §3.2 notes its order is for allocation only; Phase 1 tests run through real lowering with nested sequences |\n| 34 | Stand-in literal loses its recorded eid when a macro decodes and re-encodes it (astra P2-2) | Step 3 stand-in is an internal node type `:yin/macro-defined` recognised by shape; `:replaced` removed; validator accepts it on both sides; step 7 lowers it to a literal for `program-out`; fabricated stand-ins are inert; identity/wrapper/discard/plain-literal cases in Phase 1 |\n188:definitions  : datoms → {sym lambda-ast|nil}           ; syntactic harvest, last wins; nil = removal\n213:   root**, because harvest (step 2) reads disconnected definitions: every\n238:   `{:type :yin/macro-defined :name <sym>}` — an internal node type of the\n250:5. **Post-harvest.** Expansion can *form* definitions that did not have the\n256:   before step 2*; walk the **final tree** in **declaration order** (below);\n258:   `assoc`es and anything else `dissoc`es; at every `:yin/macro-defined\n259:   <sym>` stand-in, `assoc` the source macro `<sym>` that step 2 harvested\n261:   ignored). Last occurrence in declaration order wins. Position is a\n291:7. **Emit.** Every `:yin/macro-defined` stand-in is lowered to\n327:declaration order is). `shadow` is the set of names bound by enclosing\n475:| `:yin/macro-defined` | `:name` symbol | — (expander-internal, §3.1 step 3; accepted in operands and output, never emitted) |\n842:definition in an unexecuted branch is harvested; last definition wins;\n857:a macro fabricating a `:yin/macro-defined` for a name with no source\n858:definition this batch changes nothing; no `:yin/macro-defined` reaches\n1034:| 6 | Stale store after plain redefinition expands dead code (glm P2-3) | §3.1 harvest is authoritative for every `yin/def`: plain lambda `dissoc`es; §2.2 two-namespaces paragraph; Phase 1 test |\n1041:| 13 | \"As in Clojure\" is wrong for syntactic harvest; \"helpers are other macros\" unsupported; macro/runtime namespace relation unstated (astra P3-11) | §3.1 policy stated on its own terms; §3.3 helpers are prelude only, body expansion reserved; §2.2 two namespaces |\n1054:| 19 | `(defn m …)` after `(defmacro m …)` not caught — the `yin/def` shape appears only post-expansion; non-lambda redefinitions ignored (astra P2-2) | §3.1 step 2 `dissoc`es on any non-macro value; new step 5 post-harvest over the final tree, effective next batch; ordering rule stated |\n1057:| 22 | Admission checks only root-reachable, harvest decodes the whole batch (astra P2-5) | §3.1 step 1 checks every entity in the index |\n1068:| 28 | Post-harvest removes a later winning source macro because an earlier plain source `yin/def` survives in the tree (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 is a unified last-wins over source definitions at their positions and *generated* definitions (identified by `m` = event, never eid) at their call sites' positions; consequences enumerated; four acceptance cases plus re-parented variants in Phase 1 |\n1076:| 31 | `m` is emission provenance, not definition origin: a re-parented generated definition carries `default-op`, and an operator rewrite forms a `yin/def` under `default-op`; both escape post-harvest (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 ranks every `yin/def` occurrence in the *final tree* in traversal order, with step 3's recorded stand-in literals (`:replaced`) representing source macro definitions; no origin tracking, no use of eid or `m`; discarded-definition consequence stated; both examples in Phase 1 |\n1083:| 33 | Operator-first traversal visits `((fn [_] B) A)` as B then A, inverting `do`/`let` declaration precedence (astra P2-1) | §3.1 step 5 defines **declaration order** separately from expansion traversal: immediately-applied lambdas rank operands before body; §3.2 notes its order is for allocation only; Phase 1 tests run through real lowering with nested sequences |\n1084:| 34 | Stand-in literal loses its recorded eid when a macro decodes and re-encodes it (astra P2-2) | Step 3 stand-in is an internal node type `:yin/macro-defined` recognised by shape; `:replaced` removed; validator accepts it on both sides; step 7 lowers it to a literal for `program-out`; fabricated stand-ins are inert; identity/wrapper/discard/plain-literal cases in Phase 1 |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
